          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong Vassiliev skein relation
            is a way to extend
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariants"} knot invariants
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+knots"} singular knots
            (at least, to singular knots where the only singularities are double points). If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            is a knot invariant that takes values in an abelian group, then it is extended to singular knots using the relation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  v(L_d) = v(L_+) - v(L_-)
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_d
            is a singular knot with a double point and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_+
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_d
            by replacing the double point by a positively oriented, respectively negatively oriented, crossing.
